 "captions": [
  {
   "t": "Why does gas cost three ninety-nine and nine tenths? Who pays nine tenths of a cent?",
   "s": 0.05,
   "e": 4.48
  },
  {
   "t": "It started in the Great Depression. Gas was so cheap, some places charged under ten cents a gallon.",
   "s": 4.68,
   "e": 9.58
  },
  {
   "t": "At ten cents, raising the price by one full penny was a ten percent jump. So stations used fractions.",
   "s": 9.78,
   "e": 15.33
  },
  {
   "t": "Gas got expensive. The fraction never left. Because your brain reads three ninety-nine, not four dollars. It's called left-digit bias.",
   "s": 15.53,
   "e": 23.04
  },
  {
   "t": "And those tiny fractions add up. One estimate says around half a billion dollars a year in the U.S.",
   "s": 23.24,
   "e": 28.16
  },
  {
   "t": "Iowa actually banned it in nineteen eighty-five… and brought it back four years later.",
   "s": 28.36,
   "e": 32.43
  },
  {
   "t": "Did you ever notice the nine tenths? Follow for more price tricks.",
   "s": 32.63,
   "e": 35.87
  }
 ],
